  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nOn the leading edge of brief height rises tonight, warm advection \nand modest moisture transport will support some showers. Generally \nof the light variety, with accums <0.10\" most if not all locations. \nHeading into Sunday, short wave within the upper flow generally \npasses through and to the south of northern MI, with little in the \nway of moisture to work with during the day. Temps rise a few \ndegrees on Sunday in response to the southwest flow as sfc high \npressure centers around NJ with low pressure system taking shape \nacross the Intermountain West. \n\nEarly next week, the trough associated with the recent floods across \nthe Southwest will continue to move eastward with time, with a fast \nmoving upper low and associated jet stream ejecting on the eastern \nside of the trough. This feature will move up into the N/C \nPlains and Upper Midwest. This lead piece of energy will induce \na sfc low pressure system tracking into the Plains and eventually\nMidwest/Upper Midwest regions. Consequently, anomalous column \nmoisture will advect into northern MI during that Monday through\nWednesday time frame, with the potential for wetting rains. \nCurrent trough, sfc low, and moisture trajectory point towards \nthe healthiest rains across Upper Michigan with the lead warm\nand moist advection later Monday and Monday night, which would \ninclude the eastern UP. Potential exists for a break in the\naction before another round wetting rains possible along the\ncold front Tuesday night into Wednesday-ish. The timing and\ndetails of this will change some with time. Taking a look at \nthe Total QPF 50th percentile from all the major ensembles \n(GEPS, GEFS, and EPS, thrown in the GEFS AI and AIFS as well) \nsuggests the potential for ~0.25 to 0.75\" across northern \nlower, and ~0.50 to 1.0\" for eastern upper. This will be \ndependent on how the upper level features and subsequent sfc \nfeatures interact and ultimately migrate. Temperatures prior to \nthe eventual cold frontal passage will be in the 60s most areas,\nlikely into Wednesday. \n\nEnergy in some capacity tries to migrate/linger across the Ohio\nValley/MS Valley late week, although varying within each \ndeterministic model solution. Thus, showery activity may linger\nlate week as hinted by ensembles. The messiness of the upper \nlevel features makes this portion of the forecast quite fuzzy\nand low confidence, but at least some showers may remain late\nweek. Looks slightly cooler as well, but still above normal for\nthe first week of November.",
